   207	def _dlpack_shutdown_hook():
   208	    """atexit hook (P1T-FIX2 N5, P1T-FIX3 V3-01): close admission of GIL-taking
   209	    DLPack import releases, then wait until every release admitted before that
   210	    has finished. Interpreter finalization never starts while one is running.
   211	
   212	    The wait has no bound. After ``_DLPACK_SHUTDOWN_WAIT_S`` seconds a line goes
   213	    to stderr (how many are still running, seconds waited), and another each
   214	    time the time waited doubles; the wait goes on. Consequence: a producer
   215	    deleter that never returns makes interpreter exit wait forever. That is the
   216	    producer's DLPack contract violation (a deleter must return), and waiting is
   217	    the honest outcome: returning early would let CPython finalize under a
   218	    running callback and abort the process (round-3 review V3-01, rc -6). Ctrl-C
   219	    does not interrupt the wait; SIGTERM / SIGKILL still end the process.
   220	    Releases that start after admission closed are deliberately leaked (no GIL
   221	    is taken), as before.
   222	
   223	    P1T-FIX7 (round-7 review V7-01, sweep (i)): diagnostic configuration never
   224	    gates exit quiescence. The close is the hook's first call: the knob is read
   225	    by a plain name load (a deleted knob is caught as NameError and passes None)
   226	    and handed to the native entry as is, never converted here, so no float(),
   227	    __float__ or OverflowError can run before the close (FIX2-FIX6 called
   228	    float(knob): 1 << 4096 raised OverflowError, the close was skipped, and a
   229	    finite deleter still running on another thread aborted the process at
   230	    finalization). Everything else (the diagnostic lines, the recorded P1-T
   231	    failures) is logged after the close, each line guarded. An exception that
   232	    escapes the hook before its close (a Ctrl-C delivered at its first
   233	    bytecode, where CPython 3.12 checks for pending signals) is handled by the
   234	    native runner atexit actually calls, ``_C._dlpack_exit_hook_runner``: it
   235	    closes and waits itself, logs the recorded failures nobody reported, and
   236	    re-raises. Never raises, except a BaseException that arrived during it.
   237	    P1T-FIX8 (round-8 review V8-02): a recorded P1-T CUDA failure is reported
   238	    at exit even when its report cannot allocate. The native runner is the
   239	    last reader: after this hook (returned or raised) and after the close and
   240	    the wait, it writes, with C stdio, the records nobody raised and an R7
   241	    report this hook raised but could not write, falling back to an
   242	    allocation-free summary (count, first site, device, error name). This hook
   243	    acknowledges with _C._dlpack_exit_report_written() that its lines are out.
   244	    P1T-FIX7 (lead send-back L1): if locking the release lifecycle mutex
   245	    fails (never observed), the close retries it, and that retry wait is
   246	    diagnosed like the release wait (a stderr line after the knob's delay,
   247	    then each time the retrying time doubles; _dlpack_release_stats()
   248	    shutdown_lock_retries / shutdown_lock_diagnostics). No exit wait is
   249	    silent.
   250	    Prior art: CPython's atexit (a callback's exception is printed, the rest
   251	    still run) and Python's try / finally; see bindings.cpp.
   252	    """
   253	    C = _C
   254	    try:
   255	        knob = _DLPACK_SHUTDOWN_WAIT_S
