   692	//
   693	// P1T-FIX1, F04 (finalization-safe release). The last reference can die on any
   694	// thread, including a non-Python thread while CPython is finalizing. Once
   695	// finalization has started (or the interpreter is gone), PyGILState_Ensure is
   696	// unsafe: CPython 3.12 terminates a non-main thread that tries to take the GIL
   697	// during finalization (in the middle of this deleter), and newer runtimes may
   698	// hang it. So in that state this function does NOT call PyGILState_Ensure and
   699	// DELIBERATELY LEAKS the producer's manager (the memory is not handed back; the
   700	// process is exiting). Ordinary releases are unchanged.
   701	// Detection: Py_IsInitialized() plus Py_IsFinalizing() (3.13+) / the exported
   702	// _Py_IsFinalizing() (3.12). Measured on CPython 3.12.3 (P1T_FIX1 ledger): during
   703	// module teardown Py_IsInitialized() is already 0 and _Py_IsFinalizing() is 1,
   704	// because 3.12 clears `initialized` when it sets the finalizing thread; the
   705	// explicit finalizing test keeps this correct if a runtime orders those two
   706	// stores differently. FIX1's residual (a thread that passed this test an instant
   707	// before finalization started could still reach PyGILState_Ensure) is closed by
   708	// P1T-FIX2 N5 below.
   709	// Prior art: the CPython 3.12 C-API docs (Initialization, Finalization, and
   710	// Threads: PyGILState_Ensure / Py_IsFinalizing, "calling this function from a
   711	// thread when the runtime is finalizing will terminate the thread"); pybind11's
   712	// guidance not to touch the GIL during interpreter shutdown. Taken: the rule.
   713	// Ours: the deliberate-leak policy at this call site and its counters.
   714	//
   715	// P1T-FIX2, review finding N5 (lifecycle coordination). The check above and
   492	
   493	Storage::~Storage() {
   494	  if (external_release) {
   495	    // Imported (DLPack) memory belongs to its producer: never cudaFree it and
   496	    // never hand it to the pool. ORDER MATTERS:
   497	    //  1. Drain the engine stream. Every engine kernel, cuBLAS call and
   498	    //     cudaMemcpy runs on stream 0 (the legacy default stream; this TU is
   499	    //     built without --default-stream per-thread), so once it is idle no
   500	    //     queued engine work can still read or write this memory. The engine
   501	    //     keeps no per-Storage "last use" event, so "everything queued so far"
   502	    //     is the only sound fence; an event recorded here and waited on would
   503	    //     cover exactly the same work at three API calls instead of one. A
   504	    //     deferred release (host callback) is not allowed to run the
   505	    //     producer's deleter, which may call CUDA (torch's caching allocator).
   506	    //  2. Then hand the memory back (the DLPack deleter, GIL held, in
   507	    //     bindings.cpp). A producer may reuse it at once on its own stream.
   508	    // Prior art: the DLPack deleter contract (dmlc/dlpack, 2017+) and the
   509	    // array API's stream rules (2021+); the drain-then-delete order is the
   510	    // standard CUDA "free only after last use" rule. Ours: its placement in
   511	    // the Storage destructor.
   512	    // P1T-FIX1 F05: drain THIS Storage's device (drain_device_legacy_stream
   513	    // above); if the drain fails, deliberately leak instead of releasing.
   514	    if (device.is_cuda() && !drain_device_legacy_stream(device.index)) {
   515	      g_external_drain_leaks.fetch_add(1);
   516	      return;
   517	    }
   518	    external_release(external_ctx);
   519	    return;
   520	  }
   521	  if (!ptr) return;
   522	  if (device.is_cuda()) {
   523	    if (pooled) cudaFreeAsync(ptr, 0);
   524	    else cudaFree(ptr);
   525	  } else {
   526	    std::free(ptr);
   527	  }
   528	}
