     1	// tc_signal T5 norms (P1T-M4): InstanceNormalization, LayerNormalization and GroupNormalization-21 as one row-norm
     2	// kernel. fp32 statistics, biased variance (ONNX), deterministic, no atomic operations, no fast-math flag.
     3	//
     4	// Every norm here is "normalize each contiguous row of a C-contiguous tensor over itself, then apply an affine":
     5	//   instance_norm1d  x [N, C, T]: rows (n, c), L = T,              scale/bias entry c            (NormAffine::kPerRow)
     6	//   layer_norm       x [d0..dr): rows prod(d0..d_{axis-1}), L = prod(d_axis..), entry j (scale/bias are x.shape[axis:])
     7	//   group_norm       x [N, C, T]: rows (n, g), L = (C/G) * T,       entry g*(C/G) + j/T (per channel, GroupNorm-21)
     8	//
     9	// Statistics, per row, fp32 (shifted two-pass):
    10	//   K    = x[row, 0]                                   (the shift: any sample of the row)
    11	//   d    = (sum_j (x_j - K)) / L                       (pass 1: the mean of the shifted row)
    12	//   var  = (sum_j ((x_j - K) - d)^2) / L               (pass 2: the centred sum of squares, biased)
    13	//   rstd = 1.0f / sqrtf(var + eps)                     (math-library sqrtf, IEEE-rounded; IEEE division)
    14	//   y_j  = fmaf(((x_j - K) - d) * rstd, scale, bias)   (pass 3)
    15	// Why the shift: the row mean itself is never formed in fp32. On a row with mean 1e4 and std 1e-2 (the order's
    16	// large-offset cell) the fp32 mean is off by up to half an ulp of 1e4 = 4.9e-4 = 0.05 std, which shifts every output
    17	// by 0.05 and fails the 1e-5 gate whatever the summation order (receipt: artifacts/p1t_m4/sim_large_offset.txt).
    18	// With the shift, x_j - K is exact on such rows (Sterbenz lemma: x_j and K within a factor 2), d is a small number
    19	// computed to full relative precision, and the output error scales with the row's spread, not with its offset.
    20	//
    21	// Reduction order (fixed, so two calls are bitwise equal): one block per row (grid-stride over rows), kNormBlock
    22	// threads. Thread t sums its terms j = t, t + B, t + 2B, ... in ascending order, kNormChunk terms at a time into a partial
    23	// sum that is then added to the thread total (blocked summation), and the B thread totals are combined by a fixed
    24	// shared-memory tree (pairs at stride B/2, B/4, ..., 1). The order depends only on L, kNormBlock and kNormChunk.
    25	//
    26	// rstd choice: 1.0f / sqrtf(v) is two correctly rounded operations (<= ~1 ulp, ~1.2e-7 relative), 80x under the
    27	// order's 1e-5 normwise gate. rsqrtf (<= 2 ulp per the CUDA math documentation) would also pass; the IEEE pair is used
    28	// because the package's fast-math canary (tests/test_contract.py) already witnesses IEEE division and no flushing.
    29	//
    30	// Prior art:
    31	//  * Instance normalization: Ulyanov, Vedaldi & Lempitsky, "Instance Normalization: The Missing Ingredient for Fast
    32	//    Stylization" (2016). Layer normalization: Ba, Kiros & Hinton, "Layer Normalization" (2016). Group normalization:
    33	//    Wu & He, "Group Normalization" (2018). Semantics: ONNX InstanceNormalization, LayerNormalization (axis, epsilon,
    34	//    biased variance), GroupNormalization-21 (per-channel scale/bias, as torch.nn.GroupNorm). Taken: the definitions.
    35	//  * Variance algorithms: Welford (1962) for one-pass updating; Chan, Golub & LeVeque, "Algorithms for computing the
    36	//    sample variance: analysis and recommendations" (1983) for the two-pass algorithm, its error analysis and the
    37	//    "shifted data" variant (shift by an estimate of the mean, e.g. the first sample); Knuth, TAOCP vol. 2 §4.2.2.
    38	//    Taken: two-pass with a shift by the first sample. Ours: the CUDA mapping (one block per row, three passes over
    39	//    global memory, the shift broadcast from x[row, 0]).
    40	//  * Blocked (chunked) summation and its error bound: Higham, "Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms" (2nd ed.,
    41	//    2002), §4 (summation); Castaldo, Whaley & Chronopoulos, "Reducing floating point error in dot product using the
    42	//    superblock family of algorithms" (2008). Taken: the bound. Ours: the chunk size and its place inside a strided
    43	//    thread loop ahead of a fixed tree.
    44	//  * Deterministic shared-memory tree reduction: the standard CUDA reduction pattern (Harris, "Optimizing Parallel
    45	//    Reduction in CUDA", NVIDIA 2007). Taken: the tree. Ours: nothing beyond fixing B.
    46	//  Unverified in this sandbox (lead to check): exact titles/years above; search terms "Chan Golub LeVeque 1983 sample
    47	//  variance shifted", "Welford 1962 corrected sums of squares", "Castaldo Whaley superblock 2008".
